(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Twenty-four metformin-naive adults with metabolic syndrome completed a five-week) Tj
T*
(supervised aerobic training program while receiving either metformin \(1000 mg/day, n = 11\)) Tj
T*
(or placebo \(n = 11\). Peak oxygen uptake improvements showed a modest group difference: the) Tj
T*
(metformin group gained 0.09 L/min compared to 0.28 L/min in the placebo group \(p = 0.048\).) Tj
T*
(Relative to body weight, this difference approached but did not reach statistical) Tj
T*
(significance \(p = 0.095\). In contrast, maximal fat oxidation capacity demonstrated) Tj
T*
(substantially greater augmentation in the metformin group \(+0.15 versus +0.03 g/min, p =) Tj
T*
(0.001\). Submaximal exercise substrate oxidation patterns similarly favored the metformin) Tj
T*
(group, with significantly elevated fat utilization during moderate-intensity efforts.) Tj
T*
(Perceived exertion ratings were higher in the metformin group during high-intensity) Tj
T*
(intervals \(p = 0.019\). Age-stratified analysis revealed that metformin-induced) Tj
T*
(cardiorespiratory attenuation was more pronounced in participants older than 55 years) Tj
T*
(\(correlation r = -0.86, p < 0.001\). These findings suggest that metformin produces a) Tj
T*
(functionally distinct metabolic adaptation during aerobic training, trading aerobic) Tj
T*
(capacity for enhanced lipid utilization and greater perceived effort.) Tj
